l, Friendly Challenge to the “Weight” Idea Here’s the thought experiment I’d offer that engineer over a coffee: - A fully loaded electric motorcycle (like a LiveWire) weighs about 550 lbs. That’s more than a Smart Fortwo (which is about 1,800 lbs, so okay, bad example). Let’s use a better one: a Honda Gold Wing is 850 lbs dry. A classic Mini Cooper is about 1,300 lbs. If the sensor were truly weight-based and calibrated for even a small car, a Gold Wing should trigger it easily—but many don’t. - Conversely, you can trigger an inductive loop with a small, lightweight metal toolbox placed directly on the saw-cut. A toolbox weighs 30 lbs. If the sensor were weight-based, a 30-lb toolbox shouldn’t trip it, and an 850-lb motorcycle should. In reality, the toolbox does and the motorcycle might not. That’s the electromagnetic (non-weight) principle in action. --- So, your engineer wasn’t lying to you—they likely just described the system with a term that makes intuitive sense to everyone (“weight”) because they calibrate it against vehicle weight classes. But the underlying physics is all about metal and magnetism, not downward force. It’s a super common miscommunication even among professionals, and your motorcycle experience ironically proves the point: it’s not about being “light” in pounds; it’s about being invisible to the loop’s magnetic field.
